import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ADR-0034: Zero-Monolith, Plugin-Driven Platform","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/archived/0034-zero-monolith-plugin-driven-platform.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/archived/0034-zero-monolith-plugin-driven-platform.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/archived/0034-zero-monolith-plugin-driven-platform.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0034-zero-monolith-plugin-driven-platform" tabindex="-1">ADR-0034: Zero-Monolith, Plugin-Driven Platform <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0034-zero-monolith-plugin-driven-platform" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0034: Zero-Monolith, Plugin-Driven Platform”">​</a></h1><h2 id="status" tabindex="-1">Status <a class="header-anchor" href="#status" aria-label="Permalink to “Status”">​</a></h2><p>Accepted</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0033 unified plugin crate naming, which made the workspace navigable and the kit manifests mechanically enumerable. Naming alone is not sufficient to make the platform language-agnostic: the base framework crates still contain hard-coded canonical language assumptions, concrete value types, and monolithic statement/expression shapes. This means <code>kit-canonical</code> is not truly a curated set of plugins loaded by a generic host; it is still wired into the foundations of <code>handl-plugin-lexer</code>, <code>handl-plugin-parser</code>, <code>handl-plugin-ast</code>, <code>handl-platform</code>, <code>handl-plugin-runtime</code>, and <code>handl-plugin-target-rust</code>.</p><p>The goal of the Handl platform is to be a toolkit for arbitrary languages built from composable plugins. The canonical language is one such kit. Before we can expose non-canonical kits, the base framework must be scrubbed of all language-specific code. Only then can a kit be swapped, subset, or extended without touching the framework.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>The Handl platform will contain <strong>zero monolithic, language-specific code in the base framework crates</strong>.</p><p>The framework, plugins, kits, and hosts have the following concrete boundaries:</p><h3 id="_1-base-framework-crates-are-pure-generic-abstractions" tabindex="-1">1. Base framework crates are pure, generic abstractions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-base-framework-crates-are-pure-generic-abstractions" aria-label="Permalink to “1. Base framework crates are pure, generic abstractions”">​</a></h3><p>Base crates define traits, registries, and generic data structures. They do not know the canonical language.</p><ul><li><code>handl-plugin-lexer</code> defines <code>Token</code>, <code>Lexer</code>, and the <code>TokenKind</code> capability trait. No concrete token kind lives in the base crate.</li><li><code>handl-plugin-parser</code> defines <code>Parser</code>, <code>Grammar</code>, and the <code>GrammarRule</code> capability trait. No concrete grammar rule or canonical statement/expression shape lives in the base crate.</li><li><code>handl-plugin-ast</code> defines the generic <code>Node</code> type and per-shape capability traits. No concrete AST vocabulary lives in the base crate.</li><li><code>handl-platform</code> defines <code>Host</code>, <code>Plugin</code>, <code>RuleAcceptor</code>, <code>Operation</code>, <code>Behavior</code>, <code>ContributionContext</code>, <code>AstNode</code>, and <code>DataTypes</code>. No concrete <code>DefaultHost</code>, <code>Env</code>, or language-specific runtime value lives in the base crate.</li><li>The <code>handl-expr</code> compatibility crate has been dissolved; fixed <code>StmtKind</code> enumerations and canonical expression grammars now live in individual <code>plugin-ast-*</code> and <code>plugin-grammar-*</code> crates.</li></ul><h3 id="_2-kits-are-curated-sets-of-plugins" tabindex="-1">2. Kits are curated sets of plugins <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-kits-are-curated-sets-of-plugins" aria-label="Permalink to “2. Kits are curated sets of plugins”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>kit-canonical</code> is one kit: the built-in, configured canonical language.</li><li>A kit is a manifest plus selected default option overrides. It does not contain language implementation directly; it lists the plugins that implement the language.</li><li>New kits (e.g., <code>kit-minimal</code>, <code>kit-server</code>) can be assembled by choosing a different set of plugins without changing the framework.</li></ul><h3 id="_3-cli-and-lsp-are-hosts-not-kits" tabindex="-1">3. CLI and LSP are hosts, not kits <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-cli-and-lsp-are-hosts-not-kits" aria-label="Permalink to “3. CLI and LSP are hosts, not kits”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>handl-host-cli</code> and <code>handl-host-lsp</code> are executable hosts.</li><li>They load one or more kits and register operation <code>RuleAcceptor</code>s.</li><li>They do not hard-code the canonical language; they discover it through the kit manifest.</li></ul><h3 id="_4-vm-target-and-runtime-packages-are-tiny-composable-plugins" tabindex="-1">4. VM, target, and runtime packages are tiny composable plugins <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-vm-target-and-runtime-packages-are-tiny-composable-plugins" aria-label="Permalink to “4. VM, target, and runtime packages are tiny composable plugins”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>handl-plugin-vm</code>, <code>handl-plugin-target-*</code>, and <code>handl-plugin-runtime</code> must not contain language-specific builtin tables, concrete <code>Env</code> implementations, or value-type special cases.</li><li><code>plugin-target-rust</code> and other <code>plugin-target-*</code> crates are the only places for target-specific builtins and code generation.</li><li><code>handl-plugin-runtime</code> and <code>handl-plugin-typecheck-core</code> operate over capability traits, not a fixed <code>NonFunctionValue</code> or <code>handl-plugin-ast</code> dependency.</li><li><code>handl-plugin-vm-host-eval</code> and <code>handl-plugin-vm-backend-*</code> provide generic execution hosts; concrete value semantics are supplied by plugins.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><h3 id="positive" tabindex="-1">Positive <a class="header-anchor" href="#positive" aria-label="Permalink to “Positive”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Any language can be built by selecting plugins and composing a kit, without forking the framework.</li><li><code>handl-plugin-lexer</code> and <code>handl-plugin-parser</code> can be reused by non-canonical kits, embedded DSLs, and alternative surface syntaxes.</li><li><code>kit-canonical</code> becomes a pure, auditable manifest of the canonical language features.</li><li>CLI and LSP hosts become smaller because they delegate all language behavior to the kit.</li><li>Target, VM, and runtime packages become independently testable and replaceable.</li><li>Extension points (<code>plugin-ext-eval</code>, <code>plugin-ext-typecheck</code>, <code>plugin-ext-fmt</code>, <code>plugin-ext-lint</code>, <code>plugin-ext-build</code>, <code>plugin-ext-compile</code>) become generic operations that consume <code>Behavior</code> rules, not canonical-language special cases.</li></ul><h3 id="negative" tabindex="-1">Negative <a class="header-anchor" href="#negative" aria-label="Permalink to “Negative”">​</a></h3><ul><li>The base crates must gain richer generic extension points and trait-based registries before the canonical implementation can be fully extracted.</li><li>Many small plugins (<code>plugin-ast-*</code>, <code>plugin-grammar-*</code>, <code>plugin-token-*</code>, <code>plugin-target-*</code>) must be created or stabilized, which increases crate count and workspace maintenance.</li><li>Plugin loading, dependency resolution, and option merging become performance-critical because the host now delegates almost every language decision.</li><li>Documentation and debugging become harder until a discovery mechanism lists which plugins provide a given token, grammar rule, or runtime builtin.</li></ul><h3 id="migration-plan" tabindex="-1">Migration plan <a class="header-anchor" href="#migration-plan" aria-label="Permalink to “Migration plan”">​</a></h3><p>The audit summary produced during ADR-0033 implementation is the migration backlog. It is divided into three phases.</p><h4 id="phase-0-—-blockers-must-be-removed-before-any-non-canonical-kit-can-load" tabindex="-1">Phase 0 — Blockers (must be removed before any non-canonical kit can load) <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-0-—-blockers-must-be-removed-before-any-non-canonical-kit-can-load" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 0 — Blockers (must be removed before any non-canonical kit can load)”">​</a></h4><ul><li><code>kit-canonical</code>: remove <code>CanonicalKind</code>, <code>CanonicalFactory</code>, <code>CanonicalLexer</code>, and <code>KitParser</code> wiring from the base framework; move them into <code>plugin-token-*</code>, <code>plugin-grammar-*</code>, and <code>plugin-ast-*</code> crates.</li><li><code>handl-expr</code>: dissolved; the <code>StmtKind</code> monolith has been split into per-statement <code>plugin-ast-*</code> shapes and capability traits.</li><li><code>plugin-target-rust</code>: remove the <code>BuiltinHost</code> table and <code>RustVm</code> concrete implementation from the framework; make them target plugins.</li><li><code>handl-plugin-lexer/src/primitives.rs</code> and the default factory methods: remove canonical primitive token defaults; these are provided by <code>plugin-token-*</code> crates.</li></ul><h4 id="phase-1-—-coupled-framework-code" tabindex="-1">Phase 1 — Coupled framework code <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-1-—-coupled-framework-code" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 1 — Coupled framework code”">​</a></h4><ul><li><code>plugin-ext-typecheck</code>: remove the <code>handl-kit-type</code> direct call; query value-type capabilities through the <code>DataTypes</code> plugin.</li><li><code>plugin-ext-eval</code>: remove the <code>hello.greet</code> and JSON special-case; handle them through <code>handl-plugin-eval-hello</code> and <code>handl-plugin-vm-values-json</code>.</li><li><code>plugin-runtime/src/runtime.rs</code> and <code>plugin-runtime/src/typecheck.rs</code>: remove the <code>NonFunctionValue</code> and <code>handl-plugin-ast</code> dependencies; operate over <code>Value</code> capability traits.</li><li><code>platform/src/vm.rs</code>: remove the concrete <code>DefaultHost</code> and <code>Env</code>; replace them with <code>Host</code> plugin composition.</li></ul><h4 id="phase-2-—-cleanup-and-residual-defaults" tabindex="-1">Phase 2 — Cleanup and residual defaults <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-2-—-cleanup-and-residual-defaults" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 2 — Cleanup and residual defaults”">​</a></h4><ul><li><code>parser-common</code>: removed; its engine lives in <code>handl-plugin-parser</code> and canonical-specific parts live in <code>plugin-grammar-*</code> crates.</li><li><code>plugin-ast/src/expr.rs</code>: remove the fixed shape vocabulary; each shape becomes a <code>plugin-ast-*</code> crate.</li><li><code>plugin-ext-fmt</code>, <code>plugin-ext-lint</code>, <code>plugin-ext-build</code>, <code>plugin-ext-compile</code>: complete placeholders and ensure each is a generic <code>RuleAcceptor</code> with no canonical-language special cases.</li><li><code>plugin-lexer-common</code>: remove default rules that embed canonical defaults; move them to <code>plugin-token-*</code> crates.</li><li><code>dialect</code> crate: removed; syntax and schema vocabularies are now <code>kit-*</code> options.</li></ul><h2 id="alternatives-considered" tabindex="-1">Alternatives considered <a class="header-anchor" href="#alternatives-considered" aria-label="Permalink to “Alternatives considered”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Keep a small canonical core inside the framework</strong>: rejected because it makes every alternative language a second-class fork and prevents <code>kit-canonical</code> from being a true plugin bundle.</li><li><strong>Make the framework dynamic at runtime</strong>: rejected because we want zero-cost static dispatch and compile-time trait safety.</li><li><strong>Decompose only at the crate level, not the plugin level</strong>: rejected because it still produces monolithic grammar/AST modules that cannot be subset or reused by other kits.</li></ul><h2 id="implementation-status" tabindex="-1">Implementation status <a class="header-anchor" href="#implementation-status" aria-label="Permalink to “Implementation status”">​</a></h2><p>Implemented. The P0, P1, and P2 work items above have been completed.</p><h2 id="related-work" tabindex="-1">Related work <a class="header-anchor" href="#related-work" aria-label="Permalink to “Related work”">​</a></h2><ul><li>ADR-0001: Generic Node, Trait-Based Capabilities, and Plugin-Driven Canonical Kit</li><li>ADR-0002: Single <code>Plugin</code> Model for Host, Operations, Libraries, Targets, and Options</li><li>ADR-0033: Unified Plugin Crate Naming</li><li><code>docs/explanations/design-principles.md</code></li><li><code>kit-canonical</code> crate manifest</li></ul>`,37)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};